WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.215
A laptop provides portability,

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:05.285
while a desktop offers easier expansion and larger displays.

00:00:05.285 --> 00:00:08.400
A mini PC can serve as a compact dedicated station.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.730
A virtual machine can separate research software and files from the host,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.525
but it still depends on host security, storage, and disciplined configuration.

00:00:15.525 --> 00:00:19.220
Windows, Linux, and macOS can all support responsible OSINT work.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.760
The best choice is the platform the organization can secure, patch, back up, and support.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.880
Specialized appearance or unfamiliar complexity does not create better intelligence.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.685
Memory requirements depend on browser tabs, local document processing, mapping,

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:37.080
spreadsheets, virtual machines, and approved local AI tools.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.940
Sufficient memory prevents constant swapping and unstable multitasking.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.510
Storage should use dependable solid-state media with capacity for active cases and

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.000
recovery operations.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.820
A second display can improve note taking and source comparison,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.890
but screen privacy and physical workspace design matter.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.360
Portable systems may need privacy filters or careful placement when sensitive case

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.828
material is visible.

00:00:56.828 --> 00:01:01.320
Virtual machines are useful for snapshots, reproducible environments, and tool separation.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.280
Snapshots are not backups, and isolation is not absolute.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.035
Keep the host, hypervisor, guest system, and browsers updated,

00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:11.475
and understand where copied files, shared folders, and clipboard data are stored.

00:01:11.475 --> 00:01:13.040
Select practical reliability.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.240
Use supported hardware, tested encryption, known recovery methods,

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.740
and documented peripherals.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.165
Build enough headroom for normal workloads without buying complexity that cannot be

00:01:21.165 --> 00:01:22.280
maintained or audited.
